                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InterpretationCohomologyOfBVComplex"}
              the vanishing of compactly supported infinitesimal symmetries is equivalent to the vanishing of the cochain cohomology of the local BV-complex in degree -1
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NegativeOneCohomologyBV"} (1)
            %p
              Therefore the statement to be proven is equivalently that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+complex"} Koszul complex
              of the sequence of elements
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi δ
                              %mi EL
                            %mi L
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mrow
                            %mi Σ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi φ
                          %mrow
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    \\frac{\delta_{EL} L}{\delta \phi^a} \in \Omega^{0,0}_{\Sigma,\varphi}(E)
                    \\right)_{a = 1}^s
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+resolution"} homological resolution
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{0,0}_\Sigma(E,\varphi)\vert_{\mathcal{E}}
            %p
              By a standard fact about
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+complexes"} Koszul complexes
              (
              %a{:href => "Koszul+complex#KoszulResolutionForNoetherianRngAndElementsInJacobson"}> this prop.
              ) a sufficient condition for this to be the case is that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the ring
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mrow
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo ,
                        %mi φ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{0,0}_{\Sigma}(E,\varphi)
                  is the tensor product of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\Sigma)
                  with a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+ring"} Noetherian ring
              %li
                %p
                  the elements
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi δ
                              %mi EL
                            %mi L
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\delta_{EL} L }{\delta \phi^a}
                  are contained in its
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobson+radical"} Jacobson radical
            %p
              The first condition is the case since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{0,0}_{\Sigma}(E,\varphi)
              is by definition a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+ring"} formal power series ring
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              tensored with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\Sigma)
              (by
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "noetherian+ring#PolynomialAlgebraOverNoetherianRingIsNoetherian"} this example
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "Koszul+complex#KoszulComplexForFormalPowerSeriesAlgebras"} this example
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}
              is at least quadratic, hence that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi EL
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_{EL}\mathbf{L}
              is at least linear in the fields, guarantees that all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi δ
                          %mi EL
                        %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\delta_{EL}L}{\delta \phi^a}
              are contained in the Jacobson radical.
          %p
            Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BVComplexIsHomologicalResolutionPreciselyIfNoNonTrivialImplicitGaugeSymmetres"}
            says what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            has to accomplish: given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+BV-BRST+complex"} local BV-BRST complex
            we need to find a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            to another complex which is such that it comes from a
            %em graded
            Lagrangian density whose BV-cohomology vanishes in degree -1 and hence induces a graded covariant phase space, and such that the remaining BRST differential respects the Poisson bracket on this graded covariant phase space.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#InfinitesimalGauge
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+gauge+symmetries"} infinitesimal gauge symmetries
          %p
            Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NonTrivialImplicitInfinitesimalGaugeSymmetriesPbstructExistenceOfCauchySurfaces"}
            says that the problem is to identify the presence of spacetime-compactly supported infinitesimal symmetries that are on-shell non-trivial. One way they may be identified is if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries"} infinitesimal symmetries
            appear in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                linearly
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} parameterized collections
            %em arbitrary
            = succeed "-dependent" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            (hence is itself like a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            yields an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetry+of+the+Lagrangian"} infinitesimal symmetry of the Lagrangian
            with compact spacetime support (remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GaugeParametrizedInfinitesimalGaugeTransformation"}
            below).
          %p
            In this case we speak of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+parameter"} gauge parameter
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GaugeParameters"}
            below). It turns out that in most examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            of interest, the compactly supported infinitesimal symmetries all come from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+parameters"} gauge parameters
            this way. Therefore we now consider this case in detail.
          #GaugeParameters.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+gauge+symmetries"}> infinitesimal gauge symmetries
                )
            %p
              Let
